{"h04601-jun16-01-a":"1 see and believe is a company that specialises in computer-generated imagery (cgi) for films. producing cgi requires lots of processing power and so the company has a large number of high- performance computers. (a) explain why see and believe would use a distributed operating system. [3] question answer marks guidance 1 (a) any 2 from: 3  allow multiple computers/resources… (ao1.1 )  …to be treated as one/work on the same problem ao1.1 (ao1.1) (2) ao2.1 1 from: (1)  meaning all the computers can work on producing the same special effect. (ao2.1)","h04601-jun16-01-bi":"1 see and believe is a company that specialises in computer-generated imagery (cgi) for films. producing cgi requires lots of processing power and so the company has a large number of high- performance computers. (b) the processors in the company’s powerful computers have fast clock speeds and large amounts of cache memory. describe how each of these improves the processor’s performance: (i) fast clock speed [2] do not accept ‘ …data is processed quickly…’ as bp3 (b) (i) 2  gives more cycles per second  more instructions can be executed per second  so the program takes less time to run ao1.2 (1 per -, max 2)","h04601-jun16-01-bii":"(b) the processors in the company’s powerful computers have fast clock speeds and large amounts of cache memory. describe how each of these improves the processor’s performance: 1 see and believe is a company that specialises in computer-generated imagery (cgi) for films. producing cgi requires lots of processing power and so the company has a large number of high- performance computers. (ii) large cache memory [2] (ii) 2  more space for data/instructions in cache memory  ram needs to be accessed less frequently  accessing cache is quicker than accessing the ao1.2 ram (1 per -, max 2)","h04601-jun16-01-c":"1 see and believe is a company that specialises in computer-generated imagery (cgi) for films. producing cgi requires lots of processing power and so the company has a large number of high- performance computers. (c)* the company is working on scenes from the latest stellar scuffles film. there is strict security around the film, and there are worries about unauthorised people gaining access to the company’s network and putting clips from the film on the internet. discuss to what extent each of the following laws is intended to address the issue of someone accessing and distributing clips of the film online: • the computer misuse act • the copyright design and patents act • the data protection act. [9] (c) mark band 3–high level (7-9 marks) ao1: knowledge and understanding the candidate demonstrates a thorough knowledge and the following is indicative of possible that candidates may refer to but is not 9 understanding of to what extent all three of the laws prescriptive or exhaustive: apply to this scenario; the material is generally accurate a01.1 and detailed. the candidate is able to apply their any individual or organisation that produces media has their ownership of (2) knowledge and understanding directly and consistently to that media protected by the copyright, designs and patents act. (accept the context provided. evidence/examples will be explicitly copyright act) ao1.2 relevant to the explanation. the candidate provides a this means other parties are not allowed to reproduce o","h04601-jun16-02-a":"2 elegant bags is a company that makes designer handbags. it has decided it wants to sell its products online. elegant bags puts its website on its servers which are given a public ip address. it also purchases the domain name elegantbagsonline.co.uk. (a) explain the automated process that takes place that allows customers to access the site when they enter the domain name into their browser. [3] the company’s handbags come in a variety of materials. it would like visitors to its website to be able to select different materials in their browser and for the image shown to then instantly change to one of a handbag made of the selected material. question answer marks guidance 2 (a) 3  (customers enter) domain name and this is sent to dns servers.  dns servers map this domain to an ip address ao1.2  if the dns server can’t resolve, it passes the (3) request (recursively) to another dns server.  dns server sends the ip address to browser…  … so it can retrieve the website from server on which it is hosted. (1 per -, max 3)","h04601-jun16-02-b":"2 elegant bags is a company that makes designer handbags. it has decided it wants to sell its products online. elegant bags puts its website on its servers which are given a public ip address. it also purchases the domain name elegantbagsonline.co.uk. (b) explain how the website developer would implement this. you are not expected to write any code. [4] (b) 4 allow alternative solution using jquery or other library/plug-ins.  page will include javascript (which allows page content to be dynamic) (ao2.2) ao2.2 accept ajax can be used to prevent all images having to be pre-loaded. (1 per -, max 1) (1) plus credit ‘replaces/changes previous picture with new picture’ with bp 3 and 4  when a material is selected (javascript) code can ao3.1 (i.e. 2 marks) be executed…(ao3.1) (3)  …the appropriate picture is shown…(ao3.1)  …and hides the original picture. (ao3.1) (1 per -, max 3)","h04601-jun16-02-ci":"2 elegant bags is a company that makes designer handbags. it has decided it wants to sell its products online. elegant bags puts its website on its servers which are given a public ip address. it also purchases the domain name elegantbagsonline.co.uk. (c) elegant bags prides itself on its ethical reputation. (i) state one ethical issue the company may have considered when designing its website. [1] (c) i) ensuring the site is accessible to all. 1 accept any other sensible consideration and measure providing the measure is ethical and not legal. or (though the two aren’t necessarily mutually exclusive). ao2.1 ensuring customers’ data is stored securely. ii) 1 measure to make site accessible: making it friendly to screen readers/using alternate text for images/taking into account colour blindness in its ao2.1 colour schemes/any sensible measure. measure to secure customers’ data: encrypting data/use of firewall/penetration testing/any sensible measure.","h04601-jun16-02-cii":"(c) elegant bags prides itself on its ethical reputation. 2 elegant bags is a company that makes designer handbags. it has decided it wants to sell its products online. elegant bags puts its website on its servers which are given a public ip address. it also purchases the domain name elegantbagsonline.co.uk. (ii) state one action the company could take to address the ethical issue identified in part (i). [1] (c) i) ensuring the site is accessible to all. 1 accept any other sensible consideration and measure providing the measure is ethical and not legal. or (though the two aren’t necessarily mutually exclusive). ao2.1 ensuring customers’ data is stored securely. ii) 1 measure to make site accessible: making it friendly to screen readers/using alternate text for images/taking into account colour blindness in its ao2.1 colour schemes/any sensible measure. measure to secure customers’ data: encrypting data/use of firewall/penetration testing/any sensible measure.","h04601-jun16-03-a":"3 a burglar alarm runs on a processor with the little man computer (lmc) instruction set. one of the instructions in the set is branch if positive (brp). (a) describe what the instruction brp does. [2] a numeric pin code entered into the burglar alarm is compared with the code stored at the memory location passcode. if the codes match, the program jumps to the part of the program labelled deactivate. if the codes do not match, the program jumps to the part of the program labelled alarm. question answer marks guidance 3 (a) 2  the program flow jumps to a (designated) do not to accept ‘…branch…’ for bp 1 label/another point in the program  if the value in the accumulator is positive. ao1.1","h04601-jun16-03-b":"3 a burglar alarm runs on a processor with the little man computer (lmc) instruction set. one of the instructions in the set is branch if positive (brp). (b) write the lmc code to meet the requirements above. (you don’t have to write the code for labels deactivate and alarm, as you can assume this has already been written elsewhere.) [4] (b) 4 example code:  inputs the pin inp  compares it with the passcode stored in memory sub passcode using sub ao3.2 brz deactivate  the correct pin results in a jump to deactivate bra alarm  incorrect pin resulting in a jump to alarm","h04601-jun16-04-a":"4 a programmer spends her spare time contributing to an open source application that converts video files from a range of formats to one which uses lossy compression. (a) describe what is meant by the term ‘open source software’. [2] question answer marks guidance 4 (a) 2  source code is freely available…  …for others to amend/examine/recompile ao1.1","h04601-jun16-04-b":"4 a programmer spends her spare time contributing to an open source application that converts video files from a range of formats to one which uses lossy compression. (b) describe what is meant by the term ‘lossy compression’. [2] when a video is selected, the program gives an estimate of the file size of the converted video. the estimate in kilobytes is calculated by multiplying: • the number of pixels in the video’s resolution by… • the number of frames per second by… • the length of the video in minutes by… • the value 0.0013. (b)  (an algorithm that) makes a file storage space/ 2 size smaller …  ... but accuracy with which it represents data is ao1.1 reduced/information is lost in the process.","h04601-jun16-04-c":"4 a programmer spends her spare time contributing to an open source application that converts video files from a range of formats to one which uses lossy compression. (c) write a function in pseudocode that estimates the size of a converted video. it should: take in 3 parameters: pixels, framespersec, lengthmins • • calculate the estimated file size • return a string with the file size, including units • use megabytes for sizes under 1000 megabytes, otherwise the estimate should be given in gigabytes. examples: • 480000 pixels at 24 frames per second for 60 minutes will return a size of 898.56 mb • 480000 pixels at 24 frames per second for 120 minutes will return a size of 1.797 gb. [4] the coding team are looking at writing a new closed source version of the application that converts audio and image files in addition to video. they intend to sell copies of the program when it is complete. they investigate three programming languages they could use, including: • c++, which is compiled to machine code • java, which compiles to an intermediate code that then runs off a virtual machine • javascript, which runs from an interpreter in a web browser. (c)  function takes in all three parameters and returns a 4 example function estimatefilesize(pixels, framespersec, lengthmins) string. output = ””  calculates file size correctly. ao3.2 filesize = pixels * framespersec * lengthmins * 0.0013  files under 1000mb quoted in mb if filesize > 1000000 then  files 1000mb or over quoted in gb filesize = filesize / 1000000 output = str(filesize) + ”gb” else filesize = filesize / 1000 output = str(filesize) + ”mb” endif return output endfunction allow 1024 or 1000 kb to mb and mb to gb.","h04601-jun16-04-d":"4 a programmer spends her spare time contributing to an open source application that converts video files from a range of formats to one which uses lossy compression. (d)* discuss the benefits and drawbacks of the three options above and justify which option you would recommend. [9] (d) mark band 3–high level (7-9 marks) 9 ao1: knowledge and understanding the candidate demonstrates a thorough knowledge and the following is indicative of possible that candidates may refer to but is not u