(called total) (1) for point 1 accept ‘blank value’ for empty string and assigns it an empty / blank (string). (1) ao3.3 for point 4 accept add instead of concatenates only if it is clear it is building 2. a loop that iterates 200,000 times. (1) a string and not adding a numeric value. accept ‘append’. 3. - 4. concatenates (the string version of) j to total. (1)","h04601-jun17-03-b":"3 the following javascript has been found to crash certain web browsers. line code 1 var total = \"\"; 2 for(var j = 0; j < 200000; j++) 3 { 4 total = total + j.tostring(); 5 history.pushstate(0,0, total); 6 } j.tostring() converts j to a string. it is the javascript equivalent to str(j). (b) line 5 pushes total onto a stack. define the term stack, stating why it is suited to holding a web browser’s history. [2] (b) -a (data structure) that operates on a first in last out basis 2 accept ‘lifo’ or ‘filo’ (1) -when going back a page you want to go back to the last ao1.2 page visited/when displaying the history we want to start with the most recent first (1)","h04601-jun17-04-a":"4 a delivery company sends parcels across the uk. (a) the company charges on the following basis: parcels that have a volume of less than 0.3 m3 and weigh less than 4 kg cost £5 to send. • all other parcels cost £20 per m3 or £2 per kg, whichever is greater. • examples parcel a weighs 2.5 kg, has a volume of 0.1 m3 and costs £5 to send. parcel b weighs 6 kg, has a volume of 0.2 m3 and costs £12 to send. parcel c weighs 6 kg, has a volume of 0.8 m3 and costs £16 to send. the function getcost takes in the volume and weight of a parcel and returns the cost. getcost(2.5,0.1) returns 5 getcost(6,0.2) returns 12 getcost(6,0.8) returns 16 complete the pseudo-code below so that the function getcost returns the correct cost. function getcost(weight, volume) endfunction [4] 4 4 (a) - where weight is less than 4 and volume is less than example 0.3m3 returns 5… (1) ao3.2 function getcost(weight, volume) - …and only when both weight<4 and volume is<0.3 (1) if weight < 4 and volume < 0.3 then - where weight is >= 4 and volume >= 0.3 it returns the larger of weight * 2 if that is larger (than volume*20)… return 5 (accept getcost = 5) (1) else - …and volume * 20 if that is larger (than weight * 2). (1) cost=weight*2 if cost<volume*20 then cost=volume*20 end if return cost (accept getcost = cost) end if endfunction","h04601-jun17-04-b":"4 a delivery company sends parcels across the uk. (b) details of customers sending parcels are stored in a database. the database contains a table called parcel and a table called customer. draw an entity relationship diagram showing the parcel and customer tables. [2] 2 (b) - customer and parcel are both entities (1) ao1.2 - 1:m relationship from customer to parcel (1) (1) ao2.2 (1)","h04601-jun17-04-ci":"4 a delivery company sends parcels across the uk. (c) to prove parcels have not been damaged in transit, the delivery drivers use a digital camera to take a photograph of them when they arrive at their destination. the digital camera uses flash memory. (i) describe one advantage of the digital camera using flash storage rather than magnetic. [2] 4ci 0 1 1 0 0 0 0 2","h04601-jun17-04-cii":"(c) to prove parcels have not been damaged in transit, the delivery drivers use a digital camera to take a photograph of them when they arrive at their destination. the digital camera uses flash memory. 4 a delivery company sends parcels across the uk. (ii) explain whether lossless or lossy compression would be most appropriate to store the photographs. justify your response. [3] 4cii 0 1 2 0 0 0 0 3 5am 0 0 1 0 0 0 0 1 5bim 0 0 1 0 0 0 0 1 5cm 0 0 2 0 0 0 0 2 5dm 0 0 3 0 0 0 0 3","h04601-jun17-05-a":"5 (a) convert the binary number 01101111 to a hexadecimal number. [1] 5 (a) 6f 1 ao2.1","h04601-jun17-05-bi":"(b) convert the denary number −19 to an 8-bit number using: (i) two’s complement representation. [1] (b) i 11101101 1 ao2.1 1 ii 10010011 ao2.1","h04601-jun17-05-bii"