’s network or the internet and then route them on to the next step. the scheduling algorithm arnold’s router uses is first come first served. (i) state the name of one other scheduling algorithm. [1] 1 (b) (i) 1 mark for any of the following bullet points: 1 do not allow: • ao1.1 • round robin first come first served (1) • multi-level feedback queues • shortest job first • shortest time remaining","h04601-jun22-01-bii":"(b) arnold has a router. it will receive data packets from other computers on arnold’s network or the internet and then route them on to the next step. the scheduling algorithm arnold’s router uses is first come first served. 1 arnold has several computing devices around his home. each device has an operating system installed. (ii) explain why first come first served is a suitable scheduling algorithm for arnold’s router. [2] 1 (b) (ii) 1 mark per bullet up to a maximum of 2 marks, e.g: 2 accept processed instead of dispatched (bp1) • ao2.1 jobs dispatched on a fifo basis • (2) do not accept task/data instead of jobs (bp1 to 3) each job/packet is equal priority • each job/packet switch has the same processing time","h04601-jun22-01-c":"1 arnold has several computing devices around his home. each device has an operating system installed. (c) one role of an operating system is to manage the computer’s memory. two types of memory management are paging and segmentation. describe one difference between paging and segmentation. [2] 1 (c) 1 mark per bullet up to a maximum of 2 marks, e.g: 2 answer must cover paging and segmentation for 2 • ao1.2 marks. paging uses physical addressing…. • (2) ….segmentation uses logical addressing do not accept data instead of memory • paging uses fixed size memory blocks…. • …. segmentation uses variable length memory blocks","h04601-jun22-01-di":"1 arnold has several computing devices around his home. each device has an operating system installed. (d) different computing devices in arnold’s home use different processor arc