endering. part of the video rendering process is when the video is exported. this is when the computer combines all of the separate video elements together to form the final video. (ii)* a storage device is used to store the individual video elements while they are being captured in different locations and during the video editing process. discuss the suitability of a flash storage device and a magnetic storage device for storing the different video elements while they are being collected and edited. you should refer to the following in your answer: • the benefits of each type of storage • the drawbacks of each type of storage • the suitability of each type of storage. question answer mark guidance 1 (e) (ii) ao1.1 ao1 mark band 3–high level (2) magnetic benefits: (7-9 marks) ao1.2 • lower price per gb the candidate will provide a range of accurate benefits and (2) • as standard comes in high capacity drawbacks of both magnetic and flash devices. ao2.1 magnetic drawbacks: the candidate is able to apply their knowledge and (2) • lower r/w speed understanding directly and consistently to the context provided. ao3.3 • higher power consumption evidence/examples will be explicitly relevant to the explanation. (3) • moving parts /less durable the candidate will come to a clear conclusion that must be flash benefits: justified by their comments • higher r/w speed • no moving parts/more durable there is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and • low","h04601-jun23-02-ai":"2 a programmer uses a queue data structure to store data. (a) (i) tick one box that describes how a queue operates. last in first out first in first out [1] 2 (a) (i) ao1.1 ☑ first in first out (1)","h04601-jun23-02-aii":"2 a programmer uses a queue data structure to store data. (ii) the figure below shows a queue data structure that contains a list of names. alex is at the front of the queue. alex kofi ben sundip tom the operations that can be used on the queue are: • enqueue() – this will add data that is passed in as a parameter to the queue. • dequeue() – this will return the first element in the queue. show the contents of the queue after these operations have been performed: enqueue(\"charlie\") dequeue() enqueue(\"ling\") dequeue() enqueue(\"sara\") [2] 2 (a) (ii) ao2.1 accept: (2) ben sundip tom charlie ling sara ben sundip tom charlie ling sara 1 mark: adding, charlie, ling and sara in correct order 1 mark: exclusively removing alex and kofi.","h04601-jun23-02-bi":"2 a programmer uses a queue data structure to store data. (b) a stack is another type of data structure. a stack is implemented using these variables: • items – this is used to store an array that contains the data. • top – this is an integer value pointing to the last item of data that was inserted. pop() is one operation that can be performed on a stack. this will remove an item from the top of the stack, or –1 if the stack is empty. (i) complete the pseudocode function for the pop() operation. function pop() if top == …………………………… then return –1 else item = items[……………………………] top = top – …………………………… return …………………………… endif endfunction [4] 2 (b) (i) ao3.2 (4) function pop() if top == 0 then return -1 else item = items[top] top = top - 1 return item endif end function","h04601-jun23-02-bii":"(b) a stack is another type of data structure. a stack is implemented using these variables: • items – this is used to store an array that contains the data. • top – this is an integer value pointing to the last item of data that was inserted. pop() is one operation that can be performed on a stack. this will remove an item from the top of the stack, or –1 if the stack is empty. 2 a programmer uses a queue data structure to store data. (ii) a function called reverse uses a stack called thestack to reverse data that is passed in as a parameter called name. for example, the name “jack” would be returned as “kcaj” by the function. thestack uses these operations which are already defined as global scope in the program: • push() – this will add data that is passed in as a parameter to the stack. • pop() – this will remove and return the item on top of the stack. write the function reverse so that it: • accepts the name as a parameter • uses push() to add each character in the name to thestack separately • uses pop() to return each character from thestack and add it to a variable called reversename • outputs the variable reversename once all characters have been popped from thestack. you should write your function using pseudocode or program code. [7] 2 (b) (ii) • ao3.1 example solution correctly declaring the function reverse to include (3) function reverse(name) passing in name as a parameter reversename = \"\" • ao3.2 correct logic to calculate the number of pushes (4) required for namecount = 0 to name.length-1 • correct use of a loop to push all characters onto the thestack.push(name[namecount]) stack separately next namecount • creating a local variable reversename to hold the reversed string for namecount = 0 to name.length-1 reversename = reversename + thestack.pop() • correct use of a loop to pop all characters from the next namecount stack (in the reverse order) • correct logic to add each popped character to the return reversename reversename variable end function • correctly returning the reversename variable give full marks f","h04601-jun23-03-a":"3 (a) describe what is meant by the term ‘character set’. [2] question answer mark guidance 3 (a) • ao1.2 allow a list of letters /symbols list/mapping of characters (that can be understood by the hardware/software/computer) (2) • each character is given a unique binary/numeric code that is stored instead of the character.","h04601-jun23-03-bi":"(b) (i) convert the hexadecimal number 66 into a denary number. show your working. [2] • 3 (b) (i) 102 (correct answer) ao2.2 (2) • suitable working out","h04601-jun23-03-bii":"(ii) state two reasons why a programmer would prefer to use hexadecimal numbers rather than binary numbers. 1 2 [2] (ii) • ao2.2 do not accept hexadecimal is “easy” hexadecimal values are shorter than binary as 4 (2) communicate/enter etc… bits/nibble can be represented by one hex character. • hexadecimal values are faster / more reliable to communicate / enter / write down / read.","h04601-jun23-03-c":"(c) show the denary value 6.25 as a floating point binary number representing the mantissa and exponent. both of these should be stored in two’s complement representation. you should use as few bits as possible. show your working. [3] 3 (c) • ao2.2 final answer 011001 011 fixed point is 0110.01 • (3) mantissa becomes 0.11001 correct answer with any working gets full marks • exponent of three needed (represented at 011)","h04601-jun23-03-d":"(d) state the benefit of using a normalised form when representing data as a floating point number. [1] • 3 (d) allows for more accuracy/precision from the ao1.1 (1) given number of bits • the representation of each binary value is unique","h04601-jun23-04-a":"4 ocrconfectionery is a sweet manufacturing company. they want to use a relational database to store details of the orders their customers make. (a) state two benefits of using a relational database instead of a flat file database. 1 2 [2] question answer mark guidance 4 (a) • a01.2 relational data allows for less redundancy of data/less repeated data (2) • relational databases improve the consistency of data • relational databases allow for complex queries and/or searches to be performed","h04601-jun23-04-b":"4 ocrconfectionery is a sweet manufacturing company. they want to use a relational database to store details of the orders their customers make. (b) one customer can order as many different products as they like in the same order. a customer can also place as many orders as they like. one product can be ordered multiple times in the same order or ordered by multiple different customers. complete the entity relationship diagram to show the relationships between the product, customer and order entities. customer product order [2] 4 (b) 1 mark for each bullet point to max 2 marks: ao3.1 ignore any relationship between customer and product. (2) • accept any suitable alternative name for the orderline one customer to many orders • entity. many orders to many products // one order to many orderlines and many orderlines to one additional guidance: product for mp2, candidates may have avoided the use of many-to-many relationships, due to the information in part c). therefore, allow one product to many orders. solution 1: solution 2:","h04601-jun23-04-ci":"4 ocrconfectionery is a sweet manufacturing company. they want to use a relational database to store details of the orders their customers make. (c) the order table has these fields. orderid orderdate orderamount customerid productid (i) state the difference between a primary key and a foreign key. [2] 4 (c) (i) • ao1.1 accept entity for table a primary key will only appear once in a table/is a unique identifier (2) • a foreign key may appear multiple times a table/may not be unique","h04601-jun23-04-cii":"(c) the order table has these fields. orderid orderdate orderamount customerid productid 4 ocrconfectionery is a sweet manufacturing company. they want to use a relational database to store details of the orders their customers make. (ii) state one foreign key in the order table. [1] • 4 (c) (ii) customerid ao2.2 correct answer only (1) • productid","h04601-jun23-04-ciii":"(c) the order table has these fields. orderid orderdate orderamount customerid productid 4 ocrconfectionery is a sweet manufacturing company. they want to use a relational database to store details of the orders their customers make. (iii) state why customerid would not make a suitable primary key in the order table. [1] 4 (c) (iii) • ao2.2 there may be duplicate values. (1)","h04601-jun23-05-a":"5 amaya is an amateur photographer and has bought an old second-hand computer to edit her photographs. the s