e line is left inside the loop because it next counter will not then iterate every index.","h04602-jun25-02-biii":"(b) when the game starts, the pseudocode procedure initialisearray will initialise each name and type in the array to \"empty\". 01 procedure initialisearray(p1items:byref) 02 counter = 0 03 while counter < 10 04 p1items[counter, 0] = \"empty\" 05 p1items[counter, 1] = \"empty\" 06 counter = counter + 1 07 endwhile 08 endprocedure 2 players in a computer game collect items. each item has a name, e.g. “fruit” and a type, e.g. “apple”. the items for player 1 are stored in a 2d array. the array is 0-indexed. the pseudocode declaration for the array is: array p1items[10,2] the first dimension stores the item name and the second dimension stores the type. (iii) state one reason why the array should be passed by reference and not by value. [1] 2 (b) (iii) 1 mark from: 1 • the updated array is saved when the procedure ends • the procedure can access the array and change it. • it is a procedure so cannot return the updated array","h04602-jun25-02-c":"2 players in a computer game collect items. each item has a name, e.g. “fruit” and a type, e.g. “apple”. the items for player 1 are stored in a 2d array. the array is 0-indexed. the pseudocode declaration for the array is: array p1items[10,2] the first dimension stores the item name and the second dimension stores the type. (c) when a user collects an item, the name and type are added to the array p1items. at the end of the game, the name of each item collected is output. write an algorithm, using pseudocode or program code, to: • output the name of each item in the array • output a suitable message if there are no items in the array. if the item is \"empty\" it should not be output. [6] 2 (c) 1 mark each to max 6 6 answer is example only. • arrays may be written e.g. [x][0] suitable use of a loop to iterate through array • comparing each name [counter, 0] in all 10 allow coded examples e.g. array elements to \"empty\" … // while the item is not empty itemsfound = false • …outputting the name [counter, 0] if not \"empty\" for i in range(10): • suitable logic to count how many items are in the array // set flag… if p1items[i][0] != \"empty\": • checking if the total is 0 // checking flag is print(p1items[i][0]) set/not set… itemsfound = true • …outputting suitable message when there are no items in the array if itemsfound == false: print(\"no items found\") e.g. responses may assume items are added in sequence total = 0 from the first index and that as soon as an “empty” for x = 0 ","h04602-jun25-03":"3 a student has written this algorithm in pseudocode: for x = 1 to 4 item = data[x] y = x - 1 while (y >= 0) and (item < data[y]) data[y + 1] = data[y] y = y - 1 endwhile data[y + 1] = item next x test the algorithm by completing the trace table. the array data is a 1-dimensional array with 5 positions initialised with the data shown in the trace table. you should continue your answer on the next page if you need more space. data x item y 0 1 2 3 4 10 2 5 20 19 [6] additional space for question 3 if required. data x item y 0 1 2 3 4 3 1 mark each to max 6: 6 ignore spacing between values in columns provided additional values are not included. • first storing 1 in x, 2 in item and 0 in y shading shows marks. • swapping 10 and 2, y set to -1 and x incremented to 2 if an error occurs, or additional statement/value • storing 5 in item and 1 in y included, do not award mark where this first occurs and • swapping 10 and 5, changing y to 0 then continue as mark scheme. • 3 in x, 20 in item, 2 in y (20 present/not repeated) • 4 in x with swaps etc. and nothing superfluous after data x item y 0 1 2 3 4 10 2 5 20 19 1 2 0 10 2 -1 2 5 1 10 0 5 3 20 2 20 4 19 3 20 2 19","h04602-jun25-04-a":"4* a group of four students have decided to create a computer program to help teach younger children how to write programs in a text-based language. (a) they intend to divide the program into sub programs that they will develop individually before combining them together to form the final solution. the students are considering the agile methodologies or the waterfall lifecycle. compare the use of the agile methodologies and waterfall lifecycle for the students’ project. you should include the following in your answer: • features of each software development methodology • benefits of each software development methodology in this scenario • drawbacks of each software development methodology in this scenario. [9] 4 mark band 3–high level 9 ao1: knowledge and understanding e.g. (7-9 marks) agile programming the candidate demonstrates thorough knowledge • programming is the priority and understanding of the agile methodology and • still requires planning, design, code and test waterfall lifecycle; the material is generally • testing focus accurate and detailed. • focus on end user involvement and testing the candidate is able to apply their knowledge and • create time-box that is given to end user/customer for feedback understanding directly and consistently to the waterfall context provided. evidence/examples will be • has structured stages which are followed in a linear explicitly relevant to the explanation. the candidate fashion provides a thorough discussion which is well- • move back through pr","h04602-jun25-04-b":"4* a group of four students have decided to create a computer program to help teach younger children how to write programs in a text-based language. (b) the students intend to use both black-box testing and white-box testing. give one difference between black-box testing and white-box testing. [1] 4 (b) 1 mark from e.g. 1 both sides required for mark to be awarded • black-box: tester does not know the code/algorithms white-box: tester knows the code/algorithms • black-box: tests inputs against outputs white-box: tests each path through the algorithm","h04602-jun25-05-a":"5 a programmer has been given this pseudocode algorithm. 01 do 02 data = input(\"enter the code\") 03 until data.length > 20 04 first = data.substring(0,1) 05 second = data.substring(1,1) 06 first = int(first) 07 second = int(second) 08 if first > second then 09 print(str(first) + \"wins\") 10 elseif first == second then 11 print(\"it's a draw\") 12 else 13 print(second + \"wins\") 14 endif the pseudocode algorithm calls different subroutines. (a) identify two subroutines that are called in the pseudocode algorithm. 1 2 [2] 5 (a) 1 mark each to max 2: 2 do not accept .length as this is an attribute and not a • method input • print allow data.substring • int • str • substring","h04602-jun25-05-b":"5 a programmer has been given this pseudocode algorithm. 01 do 02 data = input(\"enter the code\") 03 until data.length > 20 04 first = data.substring(0,1) 05 second = data.substring(1,1) 06 first = int(first) 07 second = int(second) 08 if first > second then 09 print(str(first) + \"wins\") 10 elseif first == second then 11 print(\"it's a draw\") 12 else 13 print(second + \"wins\") 14 endif the pseudocode algorithm calls different subroutines. (b) name the programming constructs that start on line 01 and line 08 in the pseudocode algorithm. 01 08 [2] 5 (b) 1 mark each to max 2: 2 do not accept if statement, for loop etc. type of • 01 – iteration // looping construct not example of construct required. • 08 - branching // selection","h04602-jun25-05-c":"5 a programmer has been given this pseudocode algorithm. 01 do 02 data = input(\"enter the code\") 03 until data.length > 20 04 first = data.substring(0,1) 05 second = data.substring(1,1) 06 first = int(first) 07 second = int(second) 08 if first > second then 09 print(str(first) + \"wins\") 10 elseif first == second then 11 print(\"it's a draw\") 12 else 13 print(second + \"wins\") 14 endif the pseudocode algorithm calls different subroutines. (c) state the purpose of line 06. [1] 5 (c) 1 mark for: 1 • to change the data type of first to an integer • to cast the data type of first as an integer","h04602-jun2