ifferent subroutines. (ii) one alternative search algorithm is a binary search. describe the steps in a binary search. [4] 5 (d) (ii) 1 mark each to max 4 4 allow description through code/pseudocode • calculate middle value • compare search value to the middle value • if value is smaller, discard upper half of array// change the upper bound to the middle value -1 • if value is larger discard lower half of the array// change the lower bound to the middle value +1 • repeat until either value is found or no values left to compare// lower bound>upper bound","h04602-jun25-06-a":"6 two data structures are a queue and a stack. (a) describe one difference between a queue and a stack. [2] 6 (a) 1 mark each to max 2 e.g. 2 must be full comparison for 2 marks. • queue is fifo • stack is lifo • queue inserts data and removes data at different ends • stack inserts and removes at the same end • queues need two pointer values • stacks (usually) need one pointer value • queue can be circular • stack can only be linear","h04602-jun25-06-bi":"6 two data structures are a queue and a stack. (b) the current contents of a stack created from an array called stack and pointer called toppointer are shown: index stack 7 6 5 4 3 2 13 1 21 0 10 the toppointer currently stores 2. (i) state the purpose of toppointer in the stack. [1] 6 (b) (i) 1 mark for: 1 any equivalent description • points to the index of the last value put into the stack // points to the item at the top of the stack • points to the next item that will be output from the stack","h04602-jun25-06-bii":"(b) the current contents of a stack created from an array called stack and pointer called toppointer are shown: index stack 7 6 5 4 3 2 13 1 21 0 10 the toppointer currently stores 2. 6 two data structures are a queue and a stack. (ii) describe the steps that will be followed to remove an item from the stack. [3] 6 (b) (ii) 1 mark each to max 3: e.g. 3 • allow decrement toppointer check if stack is empty // check if toppointer ==