v20-02-c":"2 poppy would like to use a bubble sort to sort 250 000 numbers into order from lowest to highest. currently the first five numbers before they have been sorted are: 195 584 167 147 158 187 160 125 184 236 currently the last five numbers before they have been sorted are: 1058 19 558 1915 20 215 15 (c) bubble sorts make use of two different loops when sorting items into order. describe the two loops used and their purpose. 1 2 [4] ","h04602-nov20-02-d":"2 poppy would like to use a bubble sort to sort 250 000 numbers into order from lowest to highest. currently the first five numbers before they have been sorted are: 195 584 167 147 158 187 160 125 184 236 currently the last five numbers before they have been sorted are: 1058 19 558 1915 20 215 15 (d) state the name of one other sorting algorithm that poppy could have used. [1] ","h04602-nov20-03-ai":"3 oscar owns a taxi company. he would like a program to handle taxi bookings from customers. (a) when a customer makes a booking, they are placed into a queue data structure until a taxi driver is available. (i) explain why oscar uses a queue data structure rather than a stack data structure. [4] 3 (a) (i) 1 mark per bullet up to a maximum of 4 marks: 4 • a queue is first in first out (fifo) a02.2 • therefore bookings will be executed in the order (4) they have received • a stack is last in first out (lifo) • therefore the bookings would be executed from the most recent booking","h04602-nov20-03-aii":"(a) when a customer makes a booking, they are placed into a queue data structure until a taxi driver is available. 3 oscar owns a taxi company. he would like a program to handle taxi bookings from customers. (ii) oscar has written a procedure, enqueue, to be able to add a customer number to the queue. the queue is not circular. 01 procedure enqueue(custnumber) 02 maxelements = 10 03 if (tail + 1) > maxelements then 04 print (\"error, queue is full\") 05 else 06 head = head + 1 07 queue[head] = custnumber 08 endif 09 endprocedure state the name of the parameter used in the procedure enqueue. [1] custnumber 3 (a) (ii) 1 a03.3 (1)","h04602-nov20-03-aiii":"(a) when a customer makes a booking, they are placed into a queue data structure until a taxi driver is available. 3 oscar owns a taxi company. he would like a program to handle taxi bookings from customers. (iii) the procedure enqueue contains an error on line 06 and line 07. rewrite lines 06 and 07 of the procedure enqueue so that the queue works correctly. [2] 3 (a) (iii) 1 mark per bullet up to a maximum of 2 marks: 2 • correct logic for incrementing the tail pointer by 1 (e.g. tail = tail +1) a03.2 (2) • correct logic for adding custnumber to the tail","h04602-nov20-03-aiv":"(a) when a customer makes a booking, they are placed into a queue data structure until a taxi driver is available. 3 oscar owns a taxi company. he would like a program to handle taxi bookings from customers. (iv) identify the logical condition in the procedure enqueue that affects whether a new item can be added to the queue. [1] pointer (e.g. queue[tail]=custnumber) 3 (a) (iv) • if the tail is greater than 10 / maxelements 1 accept: not((tail + 1) > maxelements) a02.2 (1) or (tail + 1) <= maxelements or equivalent","h04602-nov20-03-bi":"3 oscar owns a taxi company. he would like a program to handle taxi bookings from customers. (b) some of oscar’s customers are rated as gold. customers who are rated as gold are given priority when they make a taxi booking. some customers rated as gold are shown here. arshad betty dave freddie harry jimmy kanwal lynn siad tommy will when a customer makes a booking, oscar will make use of a binary search to check if they are gold rated. oscar would like to know if ‘tommy’ is gold rated. (i) state the three values that will be set as the midpoints and then checked against ‘tommy’ on each iteration of the binary search. show your working here. midpoint 1 midpoint 2 midpoint 3 [3] 3 (b) (i) 1 mark per bullet up to a maximum of 3 marks: 3 • 5 (jimmy) a02.1 • 8 (siad) (3) • 9 (tommy)","h04602-nov20-03-bii":"(b) some of oscar’s customers are rated as gold. customers who are rated as gold are given priority when they make a taxi booking. some customers rated as gold are shown here. arshad betty dave freddie harry jimmy kanwal lynn siad tommy will when a customer makes a booking, oscar will make use of a binary search to check if they are gold rated. oscar would like to know if ‘tommy’ is gold rated. 3 oscar owns a taxi company. he would like a program to handle taxi bookings from customers. (ii) oscar has 75 000 customers stored in his program. describe the benefit to oscar of using binary searches in his program. benefit [2] 3 (b) (ii) 1 mark per bullet up to a maximum of 2 marks, e.g: 2 a01.2 • efficient (2) • ...as does not need to search every single element/uses divide and conquer","h04602-nov20-03-biii":"(b) some of oscar’s customers are rated as gold. customers who are rated as gold are given priority when they make a taxi booking. some customers rated as gold are shown here. arshad betty dave freddie harry jimmy kanwal lynn siad tommy will when a customer makes a booking, oscar will make use of a binary search to check if they are gold rated. oscar would like to know if ‘tommy’ is gold rated. 3 oscar owns a taxi company. he would like a program to handle taxi bookings from customers. (iii) state one other search algorithm that oscar could have used. [1] 3 (b) (iii) • linear search / serial search 1 a02.1 (1)","h04602-nov20-03-biv":"(b) some of oscar’s customers are rated as gold. customers who are rated as gold are given priority when they make a taxi booking. some customers rated as gold are shown here. arshad betty dave freddie harry jimmy kanwal lynn siad tommy will when a customer makes a booking, oscar will make use of a binary search to check if they are gold rated. oscar would like to know if ‘tommy’ is gold rated. 3 oscar owns a taxi company. he would like a program to handle taxi bookings from customers. (iv) state the pre-condition which has been met which meant that oscar did not need to use the search algorithm you stated in question 3(b)(iii). [1] 3 (b) (iv) 1 • the items are in alphabetical order / the items are sorted a02.1 (1)","h04602-nov20-04":"4 daisy is a computer technician. she is responsible for making sure all new employees are given a username to access the computer network. the rules that are followed when creating a new username are as follows: step 1: the employee’s first name is entered (e.g. roger) step 2: the employee’s surname is entered (e.g. banks) step 3: a username is then made up from: ○ their whole surname (e.g. banks) ○ the first letter of their first name (e.g. r) ○ a number 1 for example: banksr1 step 4: the username is then checked against existing usernames. this is done by calling a function existingusers. this will return true if the username is unique and false if the username already exists. step 5: if the username is unique then “username is unique” should be printed. if the username already exists then the number at the end of the username should increase by one (e.g. banksr2). step 6: steps 4 and 5 should be repeated until the username is unique. write a procedure called createusername that meets the rules of daisy’s program. you should write your procedure using pseudocode or program code. [9] 4 1 mark per bullet up to a maximum of 9 marks: 9 example solution: • defining the createusername procedure procedure createusername() a03.1 correctly firstname = input(\"enter first name\") (3) • suitable logic for inputting the first name surname = input(\"enter surname\") number = 0 • suitable logic for inputting the surname while unique == false a03.2 • suitable logic for using the first letter of the first number = number + 1 (6) username = surname + firstname.substring(0,1) + name str(number) • suitable logic for joining the different sections of unique = existingusers(username) the username together endwhile print(\"username is unique\") • suitable logic to pass the username into the endprocedure function existingusers (eg as a parameter or global variable) there are many different ways t","h04602-nov20-05-a":"5 given the following procedure: procedure maths(number) a = (number div 10) * 10 b = a + 10 if (number – a) >= (b – number) then print(b) else print(a) endif endprocedure (a) state the value printed by the procedure maths if number=27 [1] 5 (a) 1 • 30 a03.2 (1)","h04602-nov20-05-b":"5 given the following procedure: procedure maths(number) a = (number div 10) * 10 b = a + 10 if (number – a) >= (b – number) then print(b) else print(a) endif endprocedure (b) state the value printed by the procedure maths if number=14 [1] 5 (b) 1 • 10 a03.2 (1)","h04602-nov20-05-c":"5 given the following procedure: procedure maths(number) a = (number div 10) * 10 b = a + 10 if (number – a) >= (b – number) then print(b) else print(a) endif endprocedure (c) state the value printed by the procedure maths if number=10 [1] 5 (c) 1 • 10 a03.2 (1)","h04602-nov20-05-d":"5 given the following procedure: procedure maths(number) a = (number div 10) * 10 b = a + 10 if (number – a) >= (b – number) then print(b) else print(a) endif endprocedure (d) state the purpose of the procedure maths. [1] 5 (d) • to round the number to the nearest 10 1 a02.1 (1)","h44601-jun17-01-ai":"1 an architect firm specialises in designing skyscrapers. (a) the firm uses high end computers with high performance cpus, gpus and large amounts of ram. (i) give one use the firm might have for gpus. [1] question assessment objectives total ao1.1 ao1.2 ao2.1 ao2.2 ao3.1 ao3.2 ao3.3 1ai 1 1","h44601-jun17-01-aii":"(a) the firm uses high end computers with high performance cpus, gpus and large amounts of ram. 1 an architect firm specialises in designing skyscrapers. (ii) describe what is meant by the term ‘ram’. [2] 1aii 2 2","h44601-jun17-01-aiii":"(a) the firm uses high end computers with high performance cpus,