 dat 100 twenty dat 20 donation dat 0 total dat 0 fig. 1 1 a charity uses a desktop computer to record financial donations that it receives. the computer contains a single core, 2.4ghz processor with 2mb cache. (iii) this program is run on a processor that allows pipelining. define the term ‘pipelining’. [3] (iii) 3 ● one instruction can be fetched while another is for bp1, allow any 2 of the 3 parts of the fde cycle being decoded… for bp2, must give the other part of the fde cycle not given in bp1 ● …and another is executed ● the output of one process/instruction is the input of the next. do not award if explaining multiple cores working on different parts of fde cycle ● concurrent processing of multiple instructions // completing multiple fde cycles at once","h44601-jun22-01-civ":"(c) fig. 1 shows assembly code written using the little man computer (lmc). the program calculates and outputs the total amount that is donated to the charity in any particular day. depending on the amount, an additional bonus may be added to each amount donated. start inp sta donation sub hundred brp bonus nobonus lda total add donation sta total out bra start bonus lda total add donation add twenty sta total out bra start hundred dat 100 twenty dat 20 donation dat 0 total dat 0 fig. 1 1 a charity uses a desktop computer to record financial donations that it receives. the computer contains a single core, 2.4ghz processor with 2mb cache. (iv) explain one benefit to the charity of using a processor that allows pipelining. [2] (iv) 2 ● more instructions can be carried out in a set amount do not allow “each instruction is quicker to execute”. of time // less time to execute the same number of instructions bp2 has to be specific to the charity e.g. processing ● increasing the speed/performance/efficiency of the more donations computer/program // quicker for the program to complete","h44601-jun22-01-di":"1 a charity uses a desktop computer to record financial donations that it receives. the c