 function, gcd, is given in pseudocode. function gcd(num1, num2) if num2 == 0 then return num1 else return gcd(num2, num1 mod num2) endif endfunction (c) the function has been rewritten using iteration instead of recursion. (i) state one benefit and one drawback of using iteration instead of recursion. benefit. drawback. [2] 1 mark for benefit, 1 mark for drawback benefit:  the program can/might run faster  cannot run out of stack space/memory  easier to trace/follow 2 ao1.1 3ci drawback: (1)  iteration can lead to lengthier code  iteration can lead to code that looks more complex / is harder to understand  some problems are more elegantly coded with a recursive solution","h44602-jun19-03-cii":"(c) the function has been rewritten using iteration instead of recursion. 3 a recursive function, gcd, is given in pseudocode. function gcd(num1, num2) if num2 == 0 then return num1 else return gcd(num2, num1 mod num2) endif endfunction (ii) complete the missing statements in this iterative version of the function. function newgcd(num1, num2) temp = 0 while (num2 != ……………………………… ) ……………………………… = num2 num2 = num1 mod ……………………………… num1 = temp endwhile return ……………………………… endfunction [4] 1 mark for each correct statement function newgcd(num1, num2) temp = 0 while (num2 != 0) 4 temp = num2 ao2.2 (2) 3cii num2 = num1 mod num2 ao3.2 (2) num1 = temp endwhile return num1 endfunction","h44602-jun19-04-a":"4 mabel is a software engineer. she is writing a computer game for a client. in the game the main character has to avoid their enemies. this becomes more difficult as the levels of the game increase. (a) mabel uses decomposition to design the program. explain how decomposition can aid the design of this program. . [2] 2 1 mark per bullet ao1.2  she can split the problem down into sub problems (1) 4a  it will creates a more manageable problem / simpler to ao2.2 understand / maintain (1)  can tackle each sub problem independently","h44602-jun19-04-bi":"4 mabel is a software engineer. she is writing a computer game for a client. in the game the main character has to avoid their enemies. this becomes more difficult as the levels of the game increase. (b) the computer game allows a user to select a character (e.g. name, gender). they can then choose a level for the game (easy, normal, challenging). the user controls their character by moving it left or right. the character can jump using space bar as an input. if the character touches one of the enemies then it loses a life. the character has to make it to the end of the level without losing all their lives. the game is designed in a modular way. (i) one sub-procedure will handle the user input. describe three other sub-procedures mabel could create for the given game description. 1 2. 3 [6] 1 mark per bullet, max 2 per sub-procedure 6 do not award any user input related procedures e.g. e.g. left/right input (but character movement ao2.1  select character (name, gender) output on screen left/right would be valid) (2)  gives the user options for choosing a character ao2.2 allow other reasonable responses from the (2) scenario e.g. generate enemy()  choose level ao3.2  give the user the choice of level (easy, normal, challenging) and (2) take the user input 4bi  touch enemy  called to determine if the character touches an enemy  lose life  remove a life, if <0 then game over  end level  move onto next level one mark for identifying sensible subroutine, 1 mark for description","h44602-jun19-04-bii":"(b) the computer game allows a user to select a character (e.g. name, gender). they can then choose a level for the game (easy, normal, challenging). the user controls their character by moving it left or right. the character can jump using space bar as an input. if the character touches one of the enemies then it loses a life. the character has to make it to the end of the level without losing all their lives. the game is designed in a modular way. 4 mabel is a software engineer. she is 