ii) 1 mark each 4 do not allow nodes to be drawn • downwards. root node 22 at the start • 13 and 14 in correct order • 5 and 8 in correct order • 36 and 55 in correct order","h44602-jun23-01-aiii":"1 a tree is one example of a data structure. (iii) describe how a leaf node is deleted from a binary search tree. [2] 1 (a) (iii) 1 mark each 2 • search/traverse tree until the required node is found • set the parent node pointer to the leaf node to null • add the deleted node to the free storage list // leave for garbage clear up","h44602-jun23-01-aiv":"1 a tree is one example of a data structure. (iv) describe how a binary search tree can be searched for a value. [4] 1 (a) (iv) 1 mark each to max 4 4 • check if the root node is equal to search value and if so…. • …return/output/report found • if value is less than root node take left subtree • if value is greater than root node take right subtree • repeat process with the subtree… • …until search value is found • …until no more branches can be travelled.","h44602-jun23-01-av":"1 a tree is one example of a data structure. (v) identify the order that the nodes will be visited in a depth-first (post-order) traversal of this binary search tree. h c p a f l t [4] 1 (a) (v) 1 mark each 4 • visiting a first… • …then visiting f, c… • …then visiting l, t, p… • …visiting h last solution: a, f,c, l,t,p, h","h44602-jun23-01-avi":"1 a tree is one example of a data structure. (vi) explain how backtracking is used in depth-first (post-order) traversals. [2] 1 (a) (vi) 1 mark each to max 2 2 candidates may use an example • from the tree in 1a(v) to illustrate when a leaf node is reached… • their response. …the traversal backtracks to the leaf’s parent node • …backtracks to last node with unvisited children if an answer gives implementational detail of how a stack is used, map to the bullet points given.","h44602-jun23-01-b":"1 a tree is one example of a data structure. (b) a graph is another type of data structure. an example graph is 