 = topstack + 1 5b return true else return false endif end function","h44602-jun24-05-c":"5 the contents of a stack are stored in the 1-dimensional array called numbers. topstack stores the index of the next free space in the stack. the array is declared with space for 100 elements. (c) write an algorithm, using pseudocode or program code, to call the function push() with the value 15 and output a message saying \"added\" if the value was successfully inserted onto the stack or \"not added\" if the stack is full. [4] 1 mark each 4 true/false comparisons must be • calling push() with parameter 15 boolean values and not strings, but allow ft after that. • … storing/using return value in selection … • … comparing true/false (may be implicit e.g. if push(15) then ) … if push() is called twice bp4 cannot • … outputting a suitable message if false and if true be awarded. e.g. 5c added = push(15) if push(15) then if added = false then print(\"added\") print(\"not added\") else else print(\"not added\") print(\"added\") endif endif","h44602-jun24-06-a":"6 the following strings are stored in an array. \"rainbow\" \"moon\" \"sun\" \"stars\" \"clouds\" \"tornado\" (a) explain how a linear search would search the array for the index that stores \"clouds\". [3] 1 mark each 3 allow answers by example from the • compare the first element (rainbow) to search item / clouds given dataset • if it is equal to the search item return index / found 6a • if it is not equal move to the next element • repeat until either search item / clouds is equal // or the end of the list has been reached","h44602-jun24-06-b":"6 the following strings are stored in an array. \"rainbow\" \"moon\" \"sun\" \"stars\" \"clouds\" \"tornado\" (b) state why a binary search cannot be used in this example. [1] 1 mark for: the data is not in order/sorted 1 6b","h44602-jun24-06-c":"6 the following strings are stored in an array. \"rainbow\" \"moon\" \"sun\" \"stars\" \"clouds\" \"tornado\" (c) show how an insertion sort will sort the given data into ascending alphabetical order. \"rainbow\" \"moon\" \"sun\" \"stars\" \"clouds\" \"tornado\" [5] 5 if candidate has given descending rainbow moon sun stars clouds tornado marking marking order, max 4. guidance guidance mp1, mp3 and mp4 are lines that rainbow moon sun stars clouds tornado show a change of values during a moon rainbow sun stars clouds tornado values change 1 mark pass. moon rainbow sun stars clouds tornado 1 mark moon rainbow stars sun clouds tornado values change 1 mark 6c mp2 and mp5 do not have to be clouds moon rainbow stars sun tornado values change 1 mark explicitly given in full if there is a clouds moon rainbow stars sun tornado 1 mark comment to identify no change occur during the pass. award no marks if not an insertion sort.","h44602-jun24-06-di":"6 the following strings are stored in an array. \"rainbow\" \"moon\" \"sun\" \"stars\" \"clouds\" \"tornado\" (d) (i) a sorting algorithm has a best time complexity of o(n). describe what is meant by the best time complexity o(n) for a sorting algorithm. [2] 1 mark each 2 • linear 6di • the time will (increase) in direct proportion to the number of items","h44602-jun24-06-dii":"6 the following strings are stored in an array. \"rainbow\" \"moon\" \"sun\" \"stars\" \"clouds\" \"tornado\" (ii) another sorting algorithm has a worst space complexity of o(log(n)). describe what is meant by the worst space complexity o(log(n)) for a sorting algorithm. [2] 1 mark each 2 • logarithmic 6dii • the additional memory space required grows at a decreasing rate as the number of items increases","h44602-jun24-06-diii":"6 the following strings are stored in an array. \"rainbow\" \"moon\" \"sun\" \"stars\" \"clouds\" \"tornado\" (iii) identify the time complexity that means the time will not change even when the number of items increases. [1] constant // o(1) 1 6diii","h44602-jun24-06-div":"6 the following strings are stored in an array. \"rainbow\" \"moon\" \"sun\" \"stars\" \"clouds\" \"tornado\" (iv) identify the space complexity that means the amount of memory (space) used will double each time a new item is included. [1] exponential // o(2n) // o(kn) 1 6div","h44602-jun24-07-a":"7 a computer game stores tasks that the player has requested. each task has: • an identification (id) number e.g. task a • a real number to be processed e.g. 123456.789 • an integer number to represent the order the tasks should be accessed e.g. 1. the task that needs to be processed the earliest is given the order number 1. two or more tasks can have the same order number. for example, two tasks can have an order number 1. (a) the data about each task needs to be stored. this will store the id number, data value and order number for a task. explain why a record data structure is suitable for this data. [2] 1 mark each to max 2 for justification 2 e.g. • can store multiple items of data under one identifier // so all the data about a 7a task can be accessed using the same identifier • can store data of different data types and this task has string, real and integers","h44602-jun24-07-bi":"7 a computer game stores tasks that the player has requested. each task has: • an identification (id) number e.g. task a • a real number to be processed e.g. 123456.789 • an integer number to represent the order the tasks should be accessed e.g. 1. the task that needs to be processed the earliest is given the order number 1. two or more tasks can have the same order number. for example, two tasks can have an order number 1. (b) the tasks will be stored in a binary search tree before they are processed. they are stored in ascending order by their order number. (i) give two characteristics of a binary search tree. 1 2 [2] 1 mark each e.g. 2 • each node can have 0, 1 or 2 child nodes // a maximum of 2 child nodes • 7bi nodes are ordered (with left nodes less than the parent and right nodes greater) • the location to which a node is added depends on its order.","h44602-jun24-07-bii":"