vertical line. 7biv","h44602-jun24-08-a":"8 a group of students are designing a racing car game. the game will allow players to enter their name and then a choice of vehicle. they will then race against other vehicles that will be controlled by the program. players will use the arrow keys to control their vehicle. (a) the students are identifying the inputs and outputs for the game. complete the table by identifying two inputs and two outputs for the game. input 1 input 2 output 1 output 2 [4] 1 mark for each input to max 2 e.g. 4 allow any feasible input/output for • entering a name scenario • selecting a vehicle • pressing arrow key to move forward • pressing arrow key to move backward • pressing arrow key to move left 8a • pressing arrow key to move right 1 mark for each output to max 2 e.g. • images of vehicles to choose from • background of area • image of other vehicles • image of controls and description of what they do","h44602-jun24-08-bi":"8 a group of students are designing a racing car game. the game will allow players to enter their name and then a choice of vehicle. they will then race against other vehicles that will be controlled by the program. players will use the arrow keys to control their vehicle. (b) the students use abstraction during the design process. (i) state what is meant by abstraction and describe how it can be used to design the racing car game. definition use [3] 1 mark for definition, 1 mark each for each example of use to max 2 (3 overall) 3 allow any reasonable examples for definition: this scenario • removal of unnecessary detail for the example use, allow 2 marks for example use: • stating a valid example of abstraction 8bi e.g. simplifying scenery with an expansion. for example, • e.g. removing internal features of a vehicle that are not needed “simplify track (1) by taking out the • e.g. simplify physics for vehicle movement bumps in the road (1)” would be given • e.g. the vehicles may not be drawn to scale two marks.","h44602-jun24-08-bii":"(b) the students use abstraction during the design process. 8 a group of students are designing a racing car game. the game will allow players to enter their name and then a choice of vehicle. they will then race against other vehicles that will be controlled by the program. players will use the arrow keys to control their vehicle. (ii) explain why it is beneficial to use abstraction when designing a computer program such as a game. [3] 1 mark each to max 2 3 do not accept a reiteration of a • simplifies the problem / algorithm / programming code definition of abstraction. • faster to create the program code • 8bii final program uses less memory/processor time • programmer can focus on core aspects of the game • completed game will be simpler for end users to understand / play","h44602-jun24-08-ci":"8 a group of students are designing a racing car game. the game will allow players to enter their name and then a choice of vehicle. they will then race against other vehicles that will be controlled by the program. players will use the arrow keys to control their vehicle. (c) (i) the group of students also use decomposition. state what is meant by decomposition. [1] splitting the problem down into smaller (sub) problems 1 8ci","h44602-jun24-08-cii":"8 a group of students are designing a racing car game. the game will allow players to enter their name and then a choice of vehicle. they will then race against other vehicles that will be controlled by the program. players will use the arrow keys to control their vehicle. (ii) describe one benefit of using decomposition when designing a computer program such as a game. [2] 1 mark each to max 2 e.g. 2 allow for other valid benefits of using • decomposition. to break the problem down into individual components • …to see which components can be tackled concurrently • identify any resusable program elements … • …to avoid creating the same algorithm twice • split the program between individuals 8cii • … so they c