ers’ circuses. (a) taylor uses computational methods to analyse the problem including abstraction. describe how taylor could use abstraction in the design of his online circus game. [3] question answer marks guidance 1 mark per bullet to max 3. 3 e.g. ao2.1 (2) • remove unnecessary details // remove character features that are not needed ao2.2 • e.g. remove the outside world 1a (1) • e.g. remove complexity from the realistic entities // simply real-life objects • e.g. simplify characters/animals/tents • e.g. by representing them with specific objects/shapes","h44602-nov21-01-bi":"1 taylor is creating an online multiplayer game where users can create accounts and build their own circus. each circus will contain characters such as clowns, animals, magicians and dancers. users can set up a new circus in the online world, purchase new characters and visit other users’ circuses. (b) taylor will make use of concurrent processing within his circus game. (i) describe what is meant by the term ‘concurrent processing’. [2] 1 mark per bullet to max 2 2 e.g. ao1.1 (2) 1bi • multiple processes being executed at the same time // appearing to happen simultaneously • giving processes a slice of the processor time • having multiple processors each carrying out a different process","h44602-nov21-01-bii":"(b) taylor will make use of concurrent processing within his circus game. 1 taylor is creating an online multiplayer game where users can create accounts and build their own circus. each circus will contain characters such as clowns, animals, magicians and dancers. users can set up a new circus in the online world, purchase new characters and visit other users’ circuses. (ii) explain why concurrent processing is needed to allow multiple users to log in and interact with game elements at the same time. [3] 1 mark per bullet to max 3 3 e.g: ao2.1 (2) • game could have large number of requests to the server at a time ao2.2 • … server needs to respond in reasonable time (1) • … having multiple processors handling the different requests would increase response time 1bii • users could override each other’s changes • … e.g. needs to handle if someone updates their circus while someone else is visiting • …use record locking to stop edits if someone else has access to data • different users will have different response times • …therefore the processor can still handle other requests • …so that the performance for other users is not affected","h44602-nov21-01-ci":"1 taylor is creating an online multiplayer game where users can create accounts and build their own circus. each circus will contain characters such as clowns, animals, magicians and dancers. users can set up a new circus in the online world, purchase new characters and visit other users’ circuses. (c) some of the characters in the game will move and interact independently. taylor is going to use graphs to plan the movements that each character can take within the game. dancergold is one character. the graph shown in fig. 1 shows the possible movements that dancergold can make. 40 b e 21 80 50 38 a 55 d 90 42 50 12 g 23 0 c 33 65 f 30 fig. 1 dancergold’s starting state is represented by node a. dancergold can take any of the paths to reach the end state represented by node g. the number on each path represents the number of seconds each movement takes. the number in bold below each node is the heuristic value from a. (i) define the term heuristic in relation to the a* algorithm. [2] 1 mark per bullet to max 2 2 e.g: ao1.1 (1) • a rule of thumb / estimate / guess ao1.2 1ci • that estimates the distance / cost from each node to the destination node (1) • to speed up the process of finding a solution • …by identify which paths to follow first","h44602-nov21-01-cii":"(c) some of the characters in the game will move and interact independently. taylor is going to use graphs to plan the movements that each character can take within the game. dancergold is one character. the graph shown in fig. 1 shows