 grid or a 5 × 20 grid. describe how the program could be written to automatically generate a new nonogram. [4] 1 mark per bullet to max 4 4 e.g. ao2.1 (2) • make use of random numbers ao2.2 • generate an x/horizontal size for the grid (2) • generate a y/vertical size for the grid 8d • loop through each row/column • …generate a number between 0 and the number of rows/columns (depending on mp4 answer) • loop through each box • …generate a 1 or 0 to store in it","h04602-jun17-01-ai":"1 a 2-dimensional (2d) array, data, holds numeric data that karl has entered. the declaration for the array is: array data[16,11] the array data, has 16 ‘rows’ and 11 ‘columns’. fig. 1.1 shows an extract from data. 0 1 2 3 … 10 0 1 5 7 12 … 36 1 3 4 15 16 … 48 2 0 0 1 3 … 10 3 12 16 18 23 … 100 … … … … … … … 15 6 10 15 25 … 96 fig. 1.1 the data in each ‘row’ is in ascending numerical order. karl needs to analyse the data. (a) karl needs to find out if a number he enters appears in a given row of the array. he is going to use a search algorithm to do this. (i) state the name of two different search algorithms that karl could consider using. 1 2 [2] question answer marks guidance 1 (a) (i) 1 mark each 2 ao1.1  binary [1] (2)  linear [1]","h04602-jun17-01-aii":"(a) karl needs to find out if a number he enters appears in a given row of the array. he is going to use a search algorithm to do this. 1 a 2-dimensional (2d) array, data, holds numeric data that karl has entered. the declaration for the array is: array data[16,11] the array data, has 16 ‘rows’ and 11 ‘columns’. fig. 1.1 shows an extract from data. 0 1 2 3 … 10 0 1 5 7 12 … 36 1 3 4 15 16 … 48 2 0 0 1 3 … 10 3 12 16 18 23 … 100 … … … … … … … 15 6 10 15 25 … 96 fig. 1.1 the data in each ‘row’ is in ascending numerical order. karl needs to analyse the data. (ii) choose one search algorithm from those you gave in part (i), and describe how this algorithm works. algorithm description [5] 1 (a) (ii) if the algorithm name does not match the description given for binary search, allow movement of upper bound and lower then max 4 marks for description. bound instead of making sub-lists binary  works on an ordered set of data [1]  find mid-point [1]  if equal to mid-point [1] o report found [1]  if less than mid-point[1] o make sub-list from left [1]  if greater than mid-point [1] o make sub-list from right [1]  repeat with sub-list until found / sub-list is empty [1] linear  can work on both ordered and unordered data sets [1]  get first element [1]  if equal [1] o report found [1]  if not equal [1] o move to next element [1]  repeat for all elements, until found / end of list reached [1]","h04602-jun17-01-b":"1 a 2-dimensional (2d) array, data, holds numeric data that karl has entered. the declaration for the array is: array data[16,11] the array data, has 16 ‘rows’ and 11 ‘columns’. fig. 1.1 shows an extract from data. 0 1 2 3 … 10 0 1 5 7 12 … 36 1 3 4 15 16 … 48 2 0 0 1 3 … 10 3 12 16 18 23 … 100 … … … … … … … 15 6 10 15 25 … 96 fig. 1.1 the data in each ‘row’ is in ascending numerical order. karl needs to analyse the data. (b) karl needs to output the median value of each ‘row’ of the array. the median is found by having all the numbers in a row in ascending numerical order, and taking the middle value. for example, in fig. 1.2 below, the median element in the row is the third element, so the median value is 7. 1 5 7 12 15 fig. 1.2 write an algorithm to output the median value of each ‘row’ of the 2d array data. [3] 1 (b) 3 1 mark per bullet ao3.2  looping through/outputting for each row [1] (3)  calculating/using the middle value [1] outputting the 6th value from the array correctly [1]  e.g. for x = 0 to 15 print(data[x,5]) next x","h04602-jun17-01-c":"1 a 2-dimensional (2d) array, data, holds numeric data that karl has entered. the declaration for the array is: array data[16,11] the array data, has 16 ‘rows’ and 11 ‘columns’. fig. 1.1 shows an extract from data. 0 1 2 3 … 10 0 1 5 7 12 … 36 1 3 4 15 16 … 48 2 0 0 1 3 … 10 3 12 16 18 23 … 100 … … … … … … … 15 6 10 15 25 … 96 fig. 1.1 the data in each ‘row’ is in ascending numerical order. karl needs to analyse the data. (c) karl needs to find the mean average of each ‘column’ of the array. the mean is calculated by adding together the numbers in the column, and dividing by the quantity of numbers in the column. for example, in fig. 1.3 the first ‘column’ mean would be: (1+3+0+12)/4 = 4 1 5 7 12 3 4 15 16 0 0 1 3 12 16 18 23 fig. 1.3 write an algorithm to output the mean value of each ‘column’ in the array data. [5] 1 (c) 5 1 mark per bullet note that the running counter for the sum in each row needs ao3.2 to be reset to get the calculating average correct.  looping through each column [1] (5)  looping through each row [1]  ...adding to a total [1]  …calculating average correctly [1] note average output must appear inside the outer loop  outputting average [1] e.g. for y = 0 to 10 total = 0 for x = 0 to 15 total = total + data[x,y] next x print(total / 16) next y","h04602-jun17-02-a":"2 a group of a-level students are working together to program a computer game. in the game, the player controls a character who moves through a virtual world. the game starts with a load-up screen. the player can select which area to move to on an on-screen map, and then they control the movements of their character using a keyboard to solve puzzles on the screen. (a) identify two inputs that the user could enter to control the character and describe each input’s function. input 1 use input 2 use [4] 2 (a) 1 mark per input, 1 for description e.g. 4 answers must relate specifically to the character control, ao2.2  w/up arrow[1] which is via a keyboard. (4)  ...to allow the character to move up  s/down arrow[1]  ...to allow the character to move down  a/left arrow[1]  ...to allow the character to move left  d/right arrow[1]  ...to allow the character to move right","h04602-jun17-02-b":"2 a group of a-level students are working together to program a computer game. in the game, the player controls a character who moves through a virtual world. the game starts with a load-up screen. the player can select which area to move to on an on-screen map, and then they control the movements of their character using a keyboard to solve puzzles on the screen. (b) the game is to be created using sub-procedures. the following table identifies and describes one sub-procedure the students could use. complete the table below, identifying three additional sub-procedures that the students could create from the description at the start of question 2. describe the purpose of each sub-procedure you have identified. sub-procedure purpose e.g. charactermovement takes the key the player pressed and moves the character in that direction 1 2 3 [6] 2 (b) 6 1 mark for sub-procedure names (to max 3) 1 for allow any reasonable sub-procedure and description for the description (to max 3) ao2.2 context (6) e.g. do not award marks for character movement.  startgame [1]  loads the game, displaying the load-up screen[1]  displaymap [1]  outputs the map onto the screen[1]  selectarea [1]  the user clicks on the area of the map and this is displayed on screen[1]  loadpuzzle [1]  loads the puzzle for the chosen area and displays it on screen [1]","h04602-jun17-02-ci":"2 a group of a-level students are working together to program a computer game. in the game, the player controls a character who moves through a virtual world. the game starts with a load-up screen. the player can select which area to move to on an on-screen map, and then they control the movements of their character using a keyboard to solve puzzles on the screen. (c) the following pseudocode algorithm is for the sub-procedure charactermovement. procedure charactermovement(inputkey:byval, characterx:byref, charactery:byref) if inputkey == 37 then characterx = characterx + 1 elseif inputkey == 38 then charactery = charactery + 1 elseif inputkey == 39 then characterx = characterx – 1 elseif inputkey == 40 then charactery = charactery – 1 endif endprocedure (i) identify the three parameters in the procedure charactermovement. 1 2 3 [3] 2 (c) (i) 3 1 mark for each parameter – case sensitive no spaces allowed in parameter names ao1.2  inputkey [1] (3)  characterx [1]  charactery [1]","h04602-jun17-02-cii":"2 a group of a-level students are working together to program a computer game. in the game, the player controls a character who moves through a virtual world. the game starts with a load-up screen. the player can select which area to move to on an on-screen map, and then they control the movements of their character using a keyboard to solve puzzles on the screen. (ii) describe the decision that is made in this procedure and how the decision affects the flow through the procedure. [3] 2 (c) (ii) 3 1 mark per bullet ao1.2  decision is based on the value of inputkey… [1] (1) ... and the values of characterx or charactery  ao2.1 are changed [1] (2)  description of a condition and what it will do e.g. if the input key equals value 37 [1], then the x coordinate is increased [1]","h04602-jun17-02-ciii":"2 a group of a-level students are working together to program a computer game. in the game, the player controls a character who moves through a virtual world. the game starts with a load-up screen. the player can select which area to move to on an on-screen map, and then they control the movements of their character using a keyboard to solve puzzles on the screen. (iii) explain why characterx and charactery are passed byref and not byval. [3] 2 (c) (iii) 1 mark per bullet to max 3 3 ao2.1  byref changes the value in the variable passed (characterx and charactery) [1]  byref passes the address/location [1]  byval only a copy of the data is passed [1]  byval the change would be lost when the procedure ended [1]","h04602-jun17-02-d":"2 a group of a-level students are working together to program a computer game. in the game, the player controls a character who moves through a virtual world. the game starts with a load-up screen. the player can select which area to move to on an on-screen map, and then they control the movements of their character using a keyboard to solve puzzles on the screen. (d)* discuss the need for, and benefits of, the students producing and using reusable program components in the development of the game. [9] 2 (d) mark band 3 – high level 9 ao1: knowledge and understanding (7-9 marks) ao1.1 the following is indicative of possible factors/evidence that (2) the candidate demonstrates thorough knowledge and the candidates may refer to but is not prescriptive or ao1.2 understanding of the need and purpose of reusable exhaustive: program components. the material is generally accurate (2)  program is split into small components ao2.1 and detailed.  can identify components that are needed in more than the candidate is able to apply their knowledge and (2) one place understanding directly and consistently to the context ao3.3  write these are independent modules (3) provided.  they can be imported into other areas of the game the candidate provides a thorough discussion which is well-balanced. eva","h04602-jun17-02-e":"2 a group of a-level students are working together to program a computer game. in the game, the player controls a character who moves through a virtual world. the game starts with a load-up screen. the player can select which area to move to on an on-screen map, and then they control the movements of their character using a keyboard to solve puzzles on the screen. (e) explain, using examples, how abstraction would be used to create the virtual world. [4] 2 (e) max 2 for description of abstraction, max 3 for examples. 4 allow any reasonable example that could be applied to the ao1.2 game description, max 2 (4)  remove unnecessary elements [1]  reduce computational resources required [1]  focus on the main purpose of program//does not detract from main purpose of program [1] examples, max 3, 1 mark per example e.g.  appearance of characters is replaced by object // a character is a stick man [1]  places on the board are replaced with shapes and place name//e.g. a square that says 'town' rather than an actual town with buildings[1]  scenery is removed // e.g. trees, rivers are not included [1]","h04602-jun17-03-a":"3 a procedure takes as input a number between 1 and 100. it calculates and outputs the square of each number starting from 1, to the number input. the square of a number is the result of multiplying a number by itself. procedure squares() do number = int(input(\"enter a number between 1 and 100\")) until number >= 1 and number <= 100 for x = 1 to number print(x * x) next x endprocedure (a) the procedure uses one programming construct twice. state whether the construct that is used twice, is iteration or branching. [1] 3 (a) iteration [1] 1 ao2.1 (1)","h04602-jun17-03-b":"3 a procedure takes as input a number between 1 and 100. it calculates and outputs the square of each number starting from 1, to the number input. the square of a number is the result of multiplying a number by itself. procedure squares() do number = int(input(\"enter a number between 1 and 100\")) until number >= 1 and number <= 100 for x = 1 to number print(x * x) next x endprocedure (b) state why the algorithm is a procedure and not a function. [1] 3 (b) it does not return a value [1] 1 ao2.1 (1)","h04602-jun17-03-ci":"3 a procedure takes as input a number between 1 and 100. it calculates and outputs the square of each number starting from 1, to the number input. the square of a number is the result of multiplying a number by itself. procedure squares() do number = int(input(\"enter a number between 1 and 100\")) until number >= 1 and number <= 100 for x = 1 to number print(x * x) next x endprocedure (c) the procedure needs to be tested. (i) describe how black box testing can be used to test a program. [3] 3 (c) (i) 1 mark per bullet 3 ao1.2  tests the expected output… [1] (3)  …based on input [1]  does not look at the code // looks only at program specification [1]","h04602-jun17-03-cii":"(c) the procedure needs to be tested. 3 a procedure takes as input a number between 1 and 100. it calculates and outputs the square of each number starting from 1, to the number input. the square of a number is the result of multiplying a number by itself. procedure squares() do number = int(input(\"enter a number between 1 and 100\")) until number >= 1 and number <= 100 for x = 1 to number print(x * x) next x endprocedure (ii) for each type of test given in the table, identify two examples of test data that can be used to test the program. test type test data 1 test data 2 normal extreme invalid [3] 3 (c) (ii) 1 mark for two suitable normal data 3 1 mark for two suitable extreme data ao2.1 1 mark for two suitable invalid data (3) normal: any values between 1 and 100 (inclusive) [1] extreme: 1, 100 [1] invalid: <1, >100, “x” [1]","h04602-jun17-04-a":"4 a program stores a queue of mathematical questions to be asked to a user. the questions are asked in the order they are added. once a question has been asked it cannot be asked again. new questions are continually added to the end of the queue. the program will use a non-circular queue, questions, (implemented using an array) to store the questions. the pointer, head, stores the index of the first element in the queue. the pointer, tail, stores the index of the last element in the queue. (a) describe why a queue is a suitable structure for this program. [3] 4 (a) 1 mark per bullet to max 3 3 e.g. ao1.2 (2)  a queue is first in first out (fifo) [1] ao2.1  the questions are retrieved in the order they are (1) stored [1]  questions can be added to the end [1]  dynamic structure... [1]  ...expands to take more questions [1]","h04602-jun17-04-bi":"4 a program stores a queue of mathematical questions to be asked to a user. the questions are asked in the order they are added. once a question has been asked it cannot be asked again. new questions are continually added to the end of the queue. the program will use a non-circular queue, questions, (implemented using an array) to store the questions. the pointer, head, stores the index of the first element in the queue. the pointer, tail, stores the index of the last element in the queue. (b) fig. 4.1 shows an example of the data in the queue. head is currently 0, tail is currently 4. \"2*3\" \"1+4\" \"3-1\" \"10/2\" \"3+6\" fig. 4.1 (i) show the contents of the queue shown in fig. 4.1, after the following code is run. add(\"6+1\") [2] 4 (b) (i) 2 \"6+1\" in the correct box. [1] speech marks present [1] ao2.1 (2) \"2*3\" \"1+4\" \"3-1\" \"10/2\" \"3+6\" \"6+1\"","h04602-jun17-04-bii":"(b) fig. 4.1 shows an example of the data in the queue. head is currently 0, tail is currently 4. \"2*3\" \"1+4\" \"3-1\" \"10/2\" \"3+6\" fig. 4.1 4 a program stores a queue of mathematical questions to be asked to a user. the questions are asked in the order they are added. once a question has been asked it cannot be asked again. new questions are continually added to the end of the queue. the program will use a non-circular queue, questions, (implemented using an array) to store the questions. the pointer, head, stores the index of the first element in the queue. the pointer, tail, stores the index of the last element in the queue. (ii) state the values stored in head and tail after the code in part (i) has run. head tail [2] 4 (b) (ii) 1 mark for head, 1 for tail 2 ao2.1 head = 0 [1] (2) tail = 5 [1]","h04602-jun17-04-c":"4 a program stores a queue of mathematical questions to be asked to a user. the questions are asked in the order they are added. once a question has been asked it cannot be asked again. new questions are continually added to the end of the queue. the program will use a non-circular queue, questions, (implemented using an array) to store the questions. the pointer, head, stores the index of the first element in the queue. the pointer, tail, stores the index of the last element in the queue. (c) complete the following algorithm, to remove, and output, the first element in the queue. procedure remove() endprocedure [4] 4 (c) 4 1 mark for pseudocode/code that meets each bullet ao3.2  checking if queue is empty [1] (4)  ...outputting message/reporting error [1]  outputting element in questions at index head [1]  increment head [1] e.g. procedure remove() if head == tail + 1 then print(\"no questions\") else print(questions[head]) head = head + 1 endif endprocedure","h04602-jun17-04-d":"4 a program stores a queue of mathematical questions to be asked to a user. the questions are asked in the order they are added. once a question has been asked it cannot be asked again. new questions are continually added to the end of the queue. the program will use a non-circular queue, questions, (implemented using an array) to store the questions. the pointer, head, stores the index of the first element in the queue. the pointer, tail, stores the index of the last element in the queue. (d) complete the following algorithm, to ask the user to input a new question and then either add it to the queue, or report that the queue is full. procedure add() maxelements = 10 endprocedure [4] 4 (d) 1 mark for pseudocode/code that meets each bullet 4 ao3.2  input a question [1] (4)  check if tail is full and outputs message/reports error [1]  increment tail [1]  adds question to tail of questions [1] e.g. procedure add() maxelements = 10 item = input(\"enter a question\") if tail == maxelements - 1 then print(\"queue is full\") else tail = tail + 1 questions[tail]=item endif endprocedure","h04602-jun18-01-a":"1 a user enters whole numbers into a computer program. each number entered is placed onto a stack. the stack is created using an array with a maximum of 20 elements. part of the array, numstack, is shown when one number has been input. index stackitem 9 8 7 6 top 1 5 4 3 2 1 0 20 the pointer, top, points to the next free space in the stack. (a) complete the diagram below to show the state of numstack after the user inputs the following numbers in the order given: 22 13 2 59 1000 index stackitem 9 8 7 6 top 5 4 3 2 1 0 20 [2] question answer/indicative content mark guidance 2 1 (a) 1 mark per bullet to max 2 ao1.2  top = 6 (allow ft if numbers entered incorrectly) (1)  numbers entered in order ao2.2 (1) index stackitem 9 8 7 6 6 5 1000 top 4 59 3 2 2 13 1 22 0 20","h04602-jun18-01-bi":"1 a user enters whole numbers into a computer program. each number entered is placed onto a stack. the stack is created using an array with a maximum of 20 elements. part of the array, numstack, is shown when one number has been input. index stackitem 9 8 7 6 top 1 5 4 3 2 1 0 20 the pointer, top, points to the next free space in the stack. (b) a function, additem, takes a number as a parameter and adds the number to the stack. the function returns true if this was successful, and false if the stack is already full. (i) give one reason why a function is used instead of a procedure in this scenario. [1] question answer/indicative content mark guidance 1 (b) (i) a procedure does not return a value / a function has to return a value 1 ao1.2 (1)","h04602-jun18-01-bii":"(b) a function, additem, takes a number as a parameter and adds the number to the stack. the function returns true if this was successful, and false if the stack is already full. 1 a user enters whole numbers into a computer program. each number entered is placed onto a stack. the stack is created using an array with a maximum of 20 elements. part of the array, numstack, is shown when one number has been input. index stackitem 9 8 7 6 top 1 5 4 3 2 1 0 20 the pointer, top, points to the next free space in the stack. (ii) the parameter can be passed by value or by reference. describe what is meant by passing a parameter by value and by reference. by value by reference [4] 1 (b) (ii) 1 mark per bullet, max 2 for by value, max 2 for by reference 4 by value: ao1.2 (4)  a local copy of the data is used  data is discarded when the subprogram exits  does not override/change the original data by reference:  memory location of data is sent  changes are made to the original data  changes remain after the subprogram exits","h04602-jun18-01-biii":"(b) a function, additem, takes a number as a parameter and adds the number to the stack. the function returns true if this was successful, and false if the stack is already full. 1 a user enters whole numbers into a computer program. each number entered is placed onto a stack. the stack is created using an array with a maximum of 20 elements. part of the array, numstack, is shown when one number has been input. index stackitem 9 8 7 6 top 1 5 4 3 2 1 0 20 the pointer, top, points to the next free space in the stack. (iii) the function additem is written but is incomplete. complete the function, additem. function additem (number) if top == …………………………………… then return false else numstack[……………………………………] = ……………………………… top = …………………………………… + 1 ………………………………………………………………………… endif endfunction [5] 1 (b) (iii) 1 mark for each completed space to max 5 5 accept numstack.length() ao2.2 instead of 20 (2) function additem (number) ao3.2 if top = 20 then (3) return false else numstack[top] = number top = top + 1 return true endif endfunction","h04602-jun18-01-biv":"(b) a function, additem, takes a number as a parameter and adds the number to the stack. the function returns true if this was successful, and false if the stack is already full. 1 a user enters whole numbers into a computer program. each number entered is placed onto a stack. the stack is created using an array with a maximum of 20 elements. part of the array, numstack, is shown when one number has been input. index stackitem 9 8 7 6 top 1 5 4 3 2 1 0 20 the pointer, top, points to the next free space in the stack. (iv) the procedure, calculate, takes each item in turn from the stack. it alternately adds then subtracts the numbers until there are none left. for example, if numstack contains: 2 6 5 12 it would perform 2 + 6 – 5 + 12 and output 15. 01 procedure calculate() 02 total = 0 03 add = true 04 if top == 0 then 05 print(\"stack empty\") 06 else 07 total = numstack[top - 1] 08 top = top – 1 09 while top != 0 10 if add == true then 11 total = total + numstack[top - 1] 12 add = false 13 else 14 total = total – numstack[top - 1] 15 add = true 16 endif 17 top = top - 1 18 endwhile 19 print(total) 20 endif 21 endprocedure complete the trace table for the procedure calculate. the current array and pointer values when the procedure is called are on the first line of the trace table. numstack 0 1 2 3 4 5 top total add output 5 20 2 6 12 8 [6] question answer/indicative content mark guidance 6 1 (b) (iv) 1 mark for each bullet to max 6 ao1.2  initialising total to 0 and add to true (3)  top = 4 and total = 8 ao2.2  total = 20 and add = false (3)  top = 3, total = 14, add = true  top 2, 1. total 16, -4, add false, true  output = -4 numstack output top 0 1 2 3 4 5 total add 5 20 2 6 12 8 0 true 4 8 20 false 3 14 true 2 16 false 1 -4 true 0 -4","h04602-jun18-02-ai":"2 a games company has developed a game called kidz arrowz. the players throw an arrow at a target board and are awarded different points depending on which circle the arrow lands. fig. 1 shows the board. 1 3 6 10 fig. 1 a computer program is required to keep track of the scores for each competition. the user will enter the number of players, and the name of each player, in that competition to a maximum of 10. the program will then ask for the score of each player in turn. each competition has 8 rounds, with each player throwing one arrow each round. the program will then display the total score of each player. (a) (i) the players are declared as a record structure: record player(string playername, integer totalscore) describe what is meant by a record structure. [2] question answer/indicative content mark guidance 2 (a) (i) 1 mark per bullet to max 2 2 ao1.1  can store multiple values under one identifier (2)  the data can be of different data types","h04602-jun18-02-aii":"2 a games company has developed a game called kidz arrowz. the players throw an arrow at a target board and are awarded different points depending on which circle the arrow lands. fig. 1 shows the board. 1 3 6 10 fig. 1 a computer program is required to keep track of the scores for each competition. the user will enter the number of players, and the name of each player, in that competition to a maximum of 10. the program will then ask for the score of each player in turn. each competition has 8 rounds, with each player throwing one arrow each round. the program will then display the total score of each player. (ii) the records for the players are stored in a 1d array. state why a 1d array is a suitable data structure for the records. [1] 2 (a) (ii) 1 mark per bullet to max 1 1 ao2.2  allows easy access/indexing/manipulation of each data item in turn (1)  1d array can hold multiple items of same data type  maximum number of array elements is known","h04602-jun18-02-aiii":"2 a games company has developed a game called kidz arrowz. the players throw an arrow at a target board and are awarded different points depending on which circle the arrow lands. fig. 1 shows the board. 1 3 6 10 fig. 1 a computer program is required to keep track of the scores for each competition. the user will enter the number of players, and the name of each player, in that competition to a maximum of 10. the program will then ask for the score of each player in turn. each competition has 8 rounds, with each player throwing one arrow each round. the program will then display the total score of each player. (iii) three data structures are arrays, records and stacks. identify one other data structure. [1] 2 (a) (iii) 1 mark per bullet to max 1 1 graph ao1.1  queue tree (1) linked list  list  tuple","h04602-jun18-02-b":"2 a games company has developed a game called kidz arrowz. the players throw an arrow at a target board and are awarded different points depending on which circle the arrow lands. fig. 1 shows the board. 1 3 6 10 fig. 1 a computer program is required to keep track of the scores for each competition. the user will enter the number of players, and the name of each player, in that competition to a maximum of 10. the program will then ask for the score of each player in turn. each competition has 8 rounds, with each player throwing one arrow each round. the program will then display the total score of each player. (b) the program is decomposed into multiple sub-programs, that each perform a specific task. the array, scores, is declared as a global array of type record: global array scores[10] of player explain why the array scores has been declared as global instead of local. [2] 2 2 (b) 1 mark per bullet to max 2 ao2.1  it does not need to be passed between subroutines (1)  it can be accessed/updated at any point/place in the program ao2.2  it allows it to be updated as a running total (1)","h04602-jun18-02-ci":"2 a games company has developed a game called kidz arrowz. the players throw an arrow at a target board and are awarded different points depending on which circle the arrow lands. fig. 1 shows the board. 1 3 6 10 fig. 1 a computer program is required to keep track of the scores for each competition. the user will enter the number of players, and the name of each player, in that competition to a maximum of 10. the program will then ask for the score of each player in turn. each competition has 8 rounds, with each player throwing one arrow each round. the program will then display the total score of each player. (c) player 1 is named johnny. in the first round johnny scores 3. johnny can be added to the array using the code: scores[0].playername = \"johnny\" scores[0].