g times the candidate is able to apply their knowledge and understanding directly and (2) ao2.1 consistently to the context provided. evidence/examples will be explicitly  only 1 process can actually happen relevant to the explanation. (2) at a time on a single core processor, ao3.3 there is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically concurrent tries to simulate multiple (3) structured. the information presented is relevant and substantiated. processes mark band ","h44602-jun17-03-ai":"3 an encryption routine reads a line of text from a file, reverses the order of the characters in the string and subtracts 10 from the ascii value of each letter, then saves the new string into the same file. the program is split into sub-procedures. three sub-procedures are described as follows: • read string from file • push each character of the string onto a stack • read and encrypt each character message (a) (i) identify one further sub-procedure that could be used in the program. [1] 3 (a) (i) 1 save string in file ao2.2 (1)","h44602-jun17-03-aii":"3 an encryption routine reads a line of text from a file, reverses the order of the characters in the string and subtracts 10 from the ascii value of each letter, then saves the new string into the same file. the program is split into sub-procedures. three sub-procedures are described as follows: • read string from file • push each character of the string onto a stack • read and encrypt each character message (ii) describe two advantages of splitting the problem into sub-procedures. 1 2 [4] 3 (a) (ii) 1 mark per bullet, max 2 per advantage to max 4 4 allow any appropriate advantages ao1.2 e.g. (4)  procedures can be re-used  no need to reprogram/saves time  program can be split between programmers  can specialise in their area  speed up completion time  as multiple procedures worked on concurrently  easy to test/debug  as each module can be tested on its own then combined.","h44602-jun17-03-b":"3 an encryption routine reads a line of text from a file, reverses the order of the characters in the string and subtracts 10 from the ascii value of each letter, then saves the new string into the same file. the program is split into sub-procedures. three sub-procedures are described as follows: • read string from file • push each character of the string onto a stack • read and encrypt each character message (b) a function, readmessage: • takes the file name as a parameter • reads and returns the line of text complete the pseudocode algorithm for readmessage: function …………………………………………………………(filename) messagefile = openread(…………………………………………………………) message = messagefile.readline() messagefile. ………………………………………………………… return ………………………………………………………… endfunction [4] 3 (b) 1 mark each 4 we are not testing pseudocode ao2.1 knowledge – answers that work but do (4) not match the pseudo code given should function readmessage(filename) still be credited full marks. messagefile = openread(filename) message = messagefile.readline() readmessage and filename messagefile.close() and message are case sensitive return message endfunction","h44602-jun17-03-c":"3 an encryption routine reads a line of text from a file, reverses the order of the characters in the string and subtracts 10 from the ascii value of each letter, then saves the new string into the same file. the program is split into sub-procedures. three sub-procedures are described as follows: • read string from file • push each character of the string onto a stack • read and encrypt each character message (c) a function, push, can be used to add a character to a stack. for example: thestack.push(\"h\") places the character h onto the stack, thestack. a procedure, pushtostack, takes a string as a parameter and pushes each character of the message onto the stack, messagestack. complete the procedure below. add comments to explain how your code works. procedure pushtostack(message) endprocedure [5] 3 (c) 5 1 mark per bullet to max 5 ao2.1  use of appropriate loop (2) ao3.2  correct end condition (length of message) (3)  correct use of .push with messagestack  accessing substring (or equivalent) correctly  appropriate comment(s) procedure pushtostack(message) for x = 0 to message.length() //loop through each //letter messagestack.push(message.substring(x,1)) //take //each character and push onto stack next x //move to next letter endprocedure","h44602-jun17-03-d":"3 an encryption routine reads a line of text from a file, reverses the order of the characters in the string and subtracts 10 from the ascii value of each letter, then saves the new string into the same file. the program is split into sub-procedures. three sub-procedures are described as follows: • read string from file • push each character of the string onto a stack • read and encrypt each character message (d) describe the steps that the program would have to take in order to encrypt the characters stored in the stack, and save them in a single variable. [5] 3 (d) 1 mark per bullet to max 5 5 accept pseudocode equivalent. ao1.2  pop element from stack (2) ao2.2  convert to ascii value (3)  subtract 10 from ascii value  convert back to character  append/concatenate with variable","h44602-jun17-04-a":"4 a data structure is shown below in fig. 4.1. a b c d x e f h j g i k n l m fig. 4.1 (a) identify the data structure shown in fig. 4.1. [1] 4 (a) 1 tree // graph (undirected) do not accept binary tree ao1.2 (1)","h44602-jun17-04-bi":"4 a data structure is shown below in fig. 4.1. a b c d x e f h j g i k n l m fig. 4.1 (b) the programmer is considering using a depth-first (post-order) traversal, or a breadth-first traversal to find the path between node a and node x. (i) explain the difference between a depth-first (post-order) and breadth-first traversal. [4] 4 (b) (i) 4 1 mark per bullet to max 4 ao1.2 (4)  depth-first goes to left child node when it can...  if there is no left child it goes to the right child  when there are no child nodes the algorithm ‘visits’ it’ and backtracks to the parent node.  breadth-first visits all nodes connected directly to start node...  then visits all nodes directly connected to each of those nodes (and then all nodes directly connected to those nodes and so on…)  depth-first uses a stack  breadth-first uses a queue","h44602-jun17-04-bii":"(b) the programmer is considering using a depth-first (post-order) traversal, or a breadth-first traversal to find the path between node a and node x. 4 a data structure is shown below in fig. 4.1. a b c d x e f h j g i k n l m fig. 4.1 (ii) show how a depth-first (post-order) traversal would find the path between node a and node x for the structure shown in fig. 4.1. [6] 4 (b) (ii) 6 1 mark per node in correct order ao2.1 dklhbg(x) (6)","h44602-jun17-04-biii":"(b) the programmer is considering using a depth-first (post-order) traversal, or a breadth-first traversal to find the path between node a and node x. 4 a data structure is shown below in fig. 4.1. a b c d x e f h j g i k n l m fig. 4.1 (iii) explain how you used backtracking in your answer to part (b)(ii). [3] 4 (b) (iii) 3 max 3 e.g. ao1.2  when a node does not have any node to visit e.g. d (2) ao2.1  the algorithm goes back to the previous visited node e.g. b (1)  to check for further nodes to visit e.g. h  this repeats until a new node can be visited, or all nodes have been visited","h44602-jun17-05-a":"5 a recursive function, calculate, is shown below: 01 function calculate(num1, num2) 02 if num1 == num2 then 03 return num1 04 elseif num1 < num2 then 05 return calculate(num1, (num2-num1)) 06 else 07 return calculate(num2, (num1-num2)) 08 endif 09 endfunction (a) identify the lines where recursion is used. [1] 5 (a) 05 and 07 1 ao2.1 (1)","h44602-jun17-05-b":"5 a recursive function, calculate, is shown below: 01 function calculate(num1, num2) 02 if num1 == num2 then 03 return num1 04 elseif num1 < num2 then 05 return calculate(num1, (num2-num1)) 06 else 07 return calculate(num2, (num1-num2)) 08 endif 09 endfunction (b) trace the algorithm, showing the steps and result when the following line is run: print(calculate(4,10)) [5] 5 (b) 1 mark for each highlighted element 5 allow trace table or any sensible ao2.1 equivalent. (5)  calculate(4,10) if 4 == 10 false elseif 4 < 10 true return calculate(4, (10-4)) return calculate(4, 6)  if 4 == 6 false elseif 4 < 6 true return calculate(4, 6-4) return calculate(4, 2)  if 4 == 2 false elseif 4 < 2 false else return calculate(2, 4-2) return calculate(2,2)  if 2 == 2 true return 2 return 2 return 2 return 2  output(2)","h44602-jun17-05-c":"5 a recursive function, calculate, is shown below: 01 function calculate(num1, num2) 02 if num1 == num2 then 03 return num1 04 elseif num1 < num2 then 05 return calculate(num1, (num2-num1)) 06 else 07 return calculate(num2, (num1-num2)) 08 endif 09 endfunction (c) re-write the function so it uses iteration instead of recursion. [4] 5 (c) 1 mark per bullet to max 4 4 ao2.1  suitable loop with correct condition (1) ao2.2  in if: overwriting num2 with num2 – num1 (1) ao3.2  in else: overwriting num1 with num2... (2)  ... overwriting num2 with num1-num2 correctly (using a temp variable) e.g. alternatively swapping values by: while num1 != num2 temp = num1 if num1 < num2 then num1 = num2 num2 = temp – num2 num2 = num2 – num1 else temp = num1 – num2 num1 = num2 num2 = temp endif endwhile","h44602-jun17-06-a":"6 a software developer is creating a virtual pet game. the user can choose the type of animal they would like as their pet, give it a name and then they are responsible for caring for that animal. the user will need to feed, play with, and educate their pet. the aim is to keep the animal alive and happy, for example if the animal is not fed over a set period of time then the pet will die. • the game tells the user how hungry or bored the animal is as a percentage (%) and the animal’s intelligence is ranked as a number between 0 and 150 (inclusive). • hunger and boredom increase by 1% with every tick of a timer. • when the feed option is selected, hunger is reduced to 0. • when the play option is selected, bored is reduced to 0. • when the read option is selected, the intelligence is increased by 0.6% of its current value. an example of the game is shown: what type of pet would you like? fox or elephant? fox what would you like to name your fox? joanne joanne’s stats are hunger: 56% bored: 85% intelligence: 20 what would you like to do with your pet? play, read or feed? fig. 1.1 (a) identify three inputs that the user will have to enter to start, and/or play the game. 1 2 3 [3] 6 (a) 3 1 mark per input to max 3 allow any reasonable input to this ao2.1 system  choice of pet (3)  pet name  feed  play  read","h44602-jun17-06-bi":"6 a software developer is creating a virtual pet game. the user can choose the type of animal they would like as their pet, give it a name and then they are responsible for caring for that animal. the user will need to feed, play with, and educate their pet. the aim is to keep the animal alive and happy, for example if the animal is not fed over a set period of time then the pet will die. • the game tells the user how hungry or bored the animal is as a percentage (%) and the animal’s intelligence is ranked as a number between 0 and 150 (inclusive). • hunger and boredom increase by 1% with every tick of a timer. • when the feed option is selected, hunger is reduced to 0. • when the play option is selected, bored is reduced to 0. • when the read option is selected, the intelligence is increased by 0.6% of its current value. an example of the game is shown: what type of pet would you like? fox or elephant? fox what would you like to name your fox? joanne joanne’s stats are hunger: 56% bored: 85% intelligence: 20 what would you like to do with your pet? play, read or feed? fig. 1.1 (b) the developer is using decomposition to design the game. (i) describe the proce